Real code:

public class CardPayment implements IPaymentStrategy {
    private static final double MIN_CARD_AMOUNT = 5.0;

    @Override
    public double process(double amountTendered) {
        if (amountTendered < MIN_CARD_AMOUNT) {
            throw new InvalidPaymentException("card payments require a minimum of " + MIN_CARD_AMOUNT);
        }
        return amountTendered;
    }
}

public class SpecialCardPayment extends CardPayment {

    private static final double SPECIAL_CARD_SURCHARGE = 0.25;

    @Override
    public double process(double amountTendered) {
        double credited = super.process(amountTendered);
        return credited - SPECIAL_CARD_SURCHARGE;
    }
}

SpecialCardPayment extends CardPayment rather than implementing IPaymentStrategy directly. What does the call to super.process(amountTendered) actually reuse?,Nothing - super.process() returns the amount unchanged in all cases,"CardPayment's 5.00 minimum check, including the exception it throws - so a special card automatically enforces the same minimum without that rule being written twice",The PaymentMethod enum lookup,The change reserve behaviour,,2,"This is the practical reason for extends rather than implements: a special card genuinely IS a card and should obey every card rule, plus one extra of its own.",1

