        double credited = super.process(amountTendered);
        return credited - SPECIAL_CARD_SURCHARGE;
    }
}

Now the customer inserts 4.99 with a special card. What happens, and does the 0.25 surcharge get applied?,4.74 is credited - the surcharge is applied and the minimum is ignored for special cards,"super.process(4.99) throws InvalidPaymentException for being under the 5.00 minimum, so the surcharge line never runs at all",4.99 is credited unchanged because the surcharge only applies above the minimum,0.0 is credited and the machine waits for more money,,2,"Because the inherited check runs first and throws, the subclass's own logic is simply never reached. Calling super first is what gives the parent the chance to veto.",2

Real code:

public class PaymentStrategyFactory {

    public IPaymentStrategy getStrategy(PaymentMethod method) {
        if (method == PaymentMethod.CASH) {
            return new CashPayment();
        } else if (method == PaymentMethod.CARD) {
            return new CardPayment();
        }
        else if (method == PaymentMethod.SPECIAL) {
            return new SpecialCardPayment();
        }
        else {
            return new UPIPayment();
        }
    }
}

public class CashBackDecorator extends PaymentDecorator {

    private static final double CASH_BACK = 0.75;

    public CashBackDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        super(wrapped);
    }

    @Override
    public double process(double amount) {
        return wrapped.process(amount) + CASH_BACK;
    }
}

CashBackDecorator exists in the project and compiles. Search the SPECIAL branch of the factory above, and every other line of the project, for where it is constructed. When does a customer actually receive cashback today?,On every special card purchase,"Never. Nothing anywhere calls 'new CashBackDecorator(...)' - the factory hands back a bare SpecialCardPayment, so the class is written but completely unused",Only when the purchase is over 5.00,Only on the second and later purchases,,2,The decorator was built but never wired in. It is dead code: deleting it today would change nothing about how the program behaves.,2

