Real code:

public interface IPaymentStrategy {

    double process(double amount);

    default boolean doesAffectChangeReserve() {
        return false;
    }
}

public abstract class PaymentDecorator implements IPaymentStrategy {

    protected final IPaymentStrategy wrapped;

    protected PaymentDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }
}

PaymentDecorator declares that it implements IPaymentStrategy but contains no process() method at all. Why does this compile?,Because IPaymentStrategy's process() has a default implementation,"Because the class is abstract - an abstract class may leave interface methods unimplemented and pass the obligation to its concrete subclasses, which is why CashBackDecorator must provide process()",Because the wrapped field satisfies the interface automatically,Because process() is inherited from Object,,2,"Only doesAffectChangeReserve() has a default

Real code:

public interface IPaymentStrategy {

    double process(double amount);

    default boolean doesAffectChangeReserve() {
        return false;
    }
}

public abstract class PaymentDecorator implements IPaymentStrategy {

    protected final IPaymentStrategy wrapped;

    protected PaymentDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }
}

// CashPayment - the only type that returns true
@Override
public boolean doesAffectChangeReserve() {
    return true;
}

PaymentDecorator forwards nothing - it holds the wrapped object but overrides neither method. Suppose someone wraps a CashPayment: new CashBackDecorator(new CashPayment()). What does doesAffectChangeReserve() return on that wrapper?,true - it is forwarded to the wrapped CashPayment,"false - the wrapper has no override of its own, so it picks up the interface's default and the wrapped CashPayment's 'true' is silently lost","It throws, because the decorator does not implement the method","true, because the default in the interface is true",,2,"A decorator must forward everything it does not deliberately change. Because this one forwards nothing, wrapping a cash payment would stop the coin reserve being updated - money would go missing with no error anywhere.",2

Real code:

public abstract class PaymentDecorator implements IPaymentStrategy {

    protected final IPaymentStrategy wrapped;

    protected PaymentDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }
}

Following on from the lost cash behaviour: what should be added to PaymentDecorator to fix it for every current and future decorator at once?,Override doesAffectChangeReserve() in CashBackDecorator to return true,Override doesAffectChangeReserve() in PaymentDecorator itself to 'return wrapped.doesAffectChangeReserve()

Real code:

public class CashBackDecorator extends PaymentDecorator {

    private static final double CASH_BACK = 0.75;

    public CashBackDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        super(wrapped);
    }

    @Override
    public double process(double amount) {
        return wrapped.process(amount) + CASH_BACK;
    }
}

process() calls wrapped.process(amount) first and then adds CASH_BACK. Suppose it were rewritten as 'return wrapped.process(amount + CASH_BACK);' instead. Using a special card and a tender of 4.50, what would change?,Nothing - addition is commutative so the result is identical,"The inherited 5.00 card minimum would now see 5.25 instead of 4.50 and would pass, so a payment that should have been rejected would be accepted using the cashback to top itself up",The customer would be credited 0.75 less,It would not compile,,2,Whether you decorate before or after delegating decides which value the wrapped object's rules get to inspect. Here the difference is between a rejected payment and an accepted one.,2

