Cashback could have been written as a fourth class, SpecialCardWithCashbackPayment extends SpecialCardPayment, instead of as a decorator. Both would work today. What does the decorator give you that the subclass would not?,Better performance at runtime,"Combinability: a second effect can wrap the first one at runtime, whereas subclassing would need a new class for every combination of effects and a class can only extend one parent","Type safety, which subclassing lacks",The ability to change the card minimum,,2,"The same reasoning as the class-24 payment example. With one effect, the subclass is genuinely simpler. The decorator earns its place from the second effect onward, and from effects that switch on and off at runtime.",2

Real code:

public abstract class PaymentDecorator implements IPaymentStrategy {

    protected final IPaymentStrategy wrapped;

    protected PaymentDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        this.wrapped = wrapped;
    }
}

public class CashBackDecorator extends PaymentDecorator {

    private static final double CASH_BACK = 0.75;

    public CashBackDecorator(IPaymentStrategy wrapped) {
        super(wrapped);
    }

    @Override
    public double process(double amount) {
        return wrapped.process(amount) + CASH_BACK;
    }
}

Given the decorator holds an IPaymentStrategy rather than a SpecialCardPayment, what can CashBackDecorator legally wrap?,"Only SpecialCardPayment, because that is what the factory uses","Any IPaymentStrategy at all - cash, card, UPI, special card, or even another decorator, since the field's type is the interface rather than a concrete class",Only classes that extend PaymentDecorator,Only classes in the payment package,,2,"Depending on the interface is what allows nesting, e.g. new CashBackDecorator(new SomeOtherDecorator(new SpecialCardPayment())). If the field were typed SpecialCardPayment, none of that would be possible.",2

