Suppose SpecialCardPayment had been left as an empty class - 'public class SpecialCardPayment extends CardPayment { }' with no surcharge and no overrides. Would wrapping it in CashBackDecorator still demonstrate anything useful?,Yes - the demonstration is identical either way,"Not really. A decorator is meant to change behaviour that already exists

Real code:

public interface IPaymentStrategy {

    double process(double amount);

    default boolean doesAffectChangeReserve() {
        return false;
    }
}

The doesAffectChangeReserve() method was added to this interface AFTER CashPayment, CardPayment and UPIPayment already existed and worked. Which of these were required as a result? (select all that apply),"Marking the new method 'default' with a body, so existing implementers still compile","An override in CashPayment, the one type whose answer differs from the default",An override in CardPayment,An override in UPIPayment,Converting IPaymentStrategy into an abstract class,"1,2",Adding a plain abstract method would have broken all three implementers at once. The default body is what allowed the interface to grow while only the one type with genuinely different behaviour had to be touched.,2

Real code:

public class CardPayment implements IPaymentStrategy {
    private static final double MIN_CARD_AMOUNT = 5.0;

    @Override
    public double process(double amountTendered) {
        if (amountTendered < MIN_CARD_AMOUNT) {
            throw new InvalidPaymentException("card payments require a minimum of " + MIN_CARD_AMOUNT);
        }
        return amountTendered;
    }
}

public class SpecialCardPayment extends CardPayment {

    private static final double SPECIAL_CARD_SURCHARGE = 0.25;

    @Override
    public double process(double amountTendered) {
        double credited = super.process(amountTendered);
        return credited - SPECIAL_CARD_SURCHARGE;
    }
}

Both classes hardcode their numbers: MIN_CARD_AMOUNT = 5.0 and SPECIAL_CARD_SURCHARGE = 0.25. The bank now wants the surcharge configurable per card issuer. What does the current inheritance-based design make awkward about that?,Nothing - a static final field can be changed at runtime,"The value is fixed in the class rather than supplied to the object, so every different surcharge would need either a new subclass or a constructor parameter that the factory has no way to supply today",Subclasses in Java cannot have constructors,It would require deleting CardPayment,,2,"This is where the decorator route quietly wins: a decorator taking the rate as a constructor argument handles any number of surcharge values with one class, while subclassing pushes you toward one class per value.",2
